<text pos="392 341.826" stroke="0.18 0.49 0.56" type="label" halign="left" valign="center" size="small">ICR</text>
<text pos="420 411" stroke="0" type="label" halign="center" valign="baseline" size="large">what we plan with</text>
<path stroke="0" pen="1.4" arrow="normal/8">
284 215 m
336 215 l
</path>
<text pos="310 237" stroke="0" type="label" halign="center" valign="baseline" size="small">one axle,</text>
<text pos="310 225" stroke="0" type="label" halign="center" valign="baseline" size="small">one wheel</text>
<text pos="56 120" stroke="0" type="label" halign="left" valign="baseline" size="small">\textbf{The rear axle is rigid}: both rear wheels sit on one shaft and never steer, so the ICR always lies on that axle, extended.</text>
<text pos="56 106" stroke="0" type="label" halign="left" valign="baseline" size="small">\textbf{The front wheels cannot share an angle}: each turns about its own radius, so the inner one must turn further than the outer one.</text>
<text pos="56 92" stroke="0" type="label" halign="left" valign="baseline" size="small">$\cot\delta_o - \cot\delta_i = w/L$. \quad With a track width $w = 0.75\,L$, a commanded $22^\circ$ is really $25.5^\circ$ inside and $19.3^\circ$ outside.</text>
<text pos="56 78" stroke="0" type="label" halign="left" valign="baseline" size="small">\textbf{The model}: replace each axle by one wheel at its centre. Only $L$ and one angle $\delta$ are left, and the ICR does not move.</text>
<text pos="56 64" stroke="0" type="label" halign="left" valign="baseline" size="small">\textbf{Why that is fair here}: one servo steers both front wheels through one linkage, commanded as one number, $-100\,\%$ to $+100\,\%$.</text>
<text pos="56 50" stroke="0" type="label" halign="left" valign="baseline" size="small">It measures nothing at the front either: neither the angle difference nor the wheel speeds. Nothing there could feed a finer model.</text>
<text pos="56 36" stroke="0" type="label" halign="left" valign="baseline" size="small">The model keeps what can be commanded and observed. What it leaves out is paid for by the safety margin of chapter 10.</text>
</page>
